## Goal
Do the samples separate by experimental condition in the gene-level expression data? A principal component analysis of the gene count matrix, with each sample plotted as a point coloured by its condition (untreated vs treated, 3 samples each), as an unsupervised check of sample-level structure.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` (native) | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib; nothing is installed). All steps
run in one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted as a batch job. Inputs are read-only.

1. Resolve `counts_gene` and `design` with `_system/resolve_artifact.py` (no hardcoded paths).
2. Read the count matrix (`gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample) and the design
   table (`sample_id`, `condition`, ...). Check that the sample columns and the design's
   `sample_id` values are the same set; stop with an error if not. Order samples as in the design.
3. Filter genes: keep a gene if its count is >= 10 in at least 3 samples (the size of the smallest
   condition group). Report how many genes were kept.
4. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`. The counts are salmon length-scaled gene counts (non-integer),
   so no integer-only transform (e.g. DESeq2 VST) is used.
5. Select features: the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples after step 4
   (the DESeq2 `plotPCA` convention).
6. PCA: `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x genes matrix, mean-centred per gene, **not**
   scaled to unit variance; all components (n_samples - 1 = 5 informative ones).
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2: one point per sample, coloured by `condition`, each point labelled with its
   sample id, axis labels carry percent variance explained, legend titled "condition".
8. Write the scores, the variance explained, and the figure under `results/`.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `results/pca_by_condition.png` | `figure` | PC1 vs PC2 scatter, points coloured by condition and labelled by sample |
| `results/pca_scores.csv` | `table` | one row per sample: `sample_id`, `condition`, `PC1`...`PC5` |
| `results/pca_variance_explained.csv` | `table` | one row per component: `component`, `variance_explained`, `cumulative` |

## Execution
Runs: batch
Expected wall time under a minute (6 samples; a few tens of thousands of genes); outputs well under 1 MB.
